  "note": "Review-only batch sheet for the S06 B3c station specimens. Their appearance was approved by the owner on 2026-10-17 under decision `s06-b3c-stations-2026-10-17` (his words: \"yes\", answering \"do these seventeen stations look like the right direction?\"); that decision binds the exact source, exporter and runtime hashes listed in `art/manifests/approvals.json`, and it authorizes no landing: every binding here is still unregistered, `activation` is false and the registry still reports `procedural_legacy`. The batch: the research station, defense platform, trade station, fighter platform and repair dock across the three families that have a mesh_for seam, plus the Legion and Syndicate shipyards - the remaining seventeen faction station rows after B3a (command stations, approved) and B3b (mining / sensor / relay). This file is not an approval record, a registry entry or a runtime manifest, and it does not select any asset in production. Each station is authored *to* its binding's legacy procedural extent - per axis and per side, not merely inside it - because the review harness compares the authored footprint against the legacy station mesh within 1 percent per axis and the legacy procedural station fills its declared box exactly. rebel:shipyard is absent because it is already delivered as the approved trio's `core` asset alliance_shipyard. The ten imperial station rows are absent because render.fleet_geometry.station_mesh dispatches them straight to _station_mesh with no mesh_for seam, so authoring them is a renderer change and an owner decision rather than an asset. The B3a command stations and the nine B3b small stations are absent: they are earlier batches. overview.spacing is 240 because a row line of seventeen station rows must not overlap: the widest pair this batch puts side by side is two 110-radius shipyards (220). overview yaw/pitch/distance are 90/40/2800, not the 48/50/1100 the earlier station batches used. Seventeen rows span 3840 units, and at a 48-degree yaw the eye is displaced 0.43 x distance *along* the row axis, so it ends up level with the far end of the line: rows beyond it are behind the camera and the near ones project past the frame edge. Measured on this spec's first run, the 48-degree plan put the far rows at negative depth and the near end at x_ndc -1.25, so the only scale view in the sheet showed four objects. At a 90-degree yaw the line is perpendicular to the view, every row sits at 2800 units and inside (-0.82 ... +0.82), and the lineup is a true single-scale comparison with no perspective spread. It is a presence-and-scale lineup; each row's own close and tactical views are its appearance evidence. The overview is a presence-and-scale lineup of all seventeen rows, not a per-row scale comparison; each row's own close and tactical views are its evidence. overview.close_distance_floor is 150: these stations frame at 110-215 units, and the shared scene's context group puts the near eye inside a hull without it - the harness refuses that capture as `camera not outside union`; the floor is inert for every earlier batch. The batch's --motion list names one row per family (rebel:research_station, confederacy:shipyard, syndicate:defense_platform) and every row that claims a clip is listed. Correction (same day, against the code): the claim above that the imperial station rows dispatch straight to _station_mesh with no mesh_for seam was wrong - render/fleet_geometry.py::station_mesh consults mesh_for(f'{faction}:{data_id}') ahead of its imperial branch for every faction, so those rows are ordinary asset work once the imperial rows are in scope (owner decision s06-imperial-migration-2026-10-17). What imperial ships need is a per-binding key in render/faction_visuals.py::ship_visual_key, which today mints one only for the three non-imperial families.",
